    "knowledge_point": "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table",
    "problem": "Consider the function $f(x,y)=-{x}^{3}(y+1).$ Fill in the missing signs in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $y>-1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |",
    "worked_solution": "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/ecbba4555023bdc89892cd902b30039e3a76a919b68c5a2bec670213dfc78c34.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/9ca3f95ecca25c73eadd7cbd3840cbc1f8336ff7a1a1598876c11bd4af16805b.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-{x}^{3}$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,0)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-2)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/e02c333ffb3063ebf0f214cf88cb9a6d486cff0db958de6d47943f3ca0739e45.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |",
